WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.620
This is the DP Pod, episode 46. I'm Dan Pere, he's Patrick Novak. You sound suspiciously well-rested for a guy who flies for a living. That's because I actually slept. No weather holds, no reroutes, boring in the best way. I'll take boring reliability over drama every single day, which honestly is the whole show. Record clean energy additions actually strengthening grid reliability, and conservation programs posting species recoveries you can count.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:35.280
Measured, not promised. That's the part I like. Time for the positive papers.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:46.860
Start with the grid. We were on the clean energy bill yesterday. What's new is Australia adding enough renewable capacity in the past year that clean generation is heading toward a majority share in several states.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:59.520
And the national tribune is not just waving a capacity number around. Utility scale wind and solar plus rooftop systems paired with the storage they already have are improving evening peak reliability.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:06.780
Grid operators now have real data showing distributed resources cutting the need for peeker plants in high demand windows.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:14.540
Wait. Several states. Toward a majority. That's the report. Okay, that's an operator number I respect.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:25.560
Peaker plants are the spare engine you only spin up when you have to. If rooftop plus storage is keeping that engine dark during the evening bank, that's not a press release. That's a dispatch longer.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:40.880
You just turned the Australian grid into a pre-flight. I love it. And I also want the magnitude not the headline percentage. Second order effects on wholesale prices only show up once storage can shove daytime surplus into those evening peaks.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:55.480
A majority share that sits in the afternoon and vanishes at six does not move the bill. See that's where I get skeptical about how big this is. One good year of additions is not the same as surviving a multi-day stretch of low wind and low sun.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:11.140
Do the reserve margins already pice that or are we celebrating a sunny season? That's a fair steel man and I'll take it. Storage cost curves and transmission bottlenecks are still wide open. Those two can stall the next wave and I should not talk like they're solved.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:21.460
And I'll give you the peeker plant data. That's measured performance in real regions not a model. State utilities and private developers actually finished projects on accelerated timelines.

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:28.960
For a regular person in those states, the honest wind is less long term price volatility, not a miracle overnight rate cut.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:38.740
The open question I keep circling is which country crosses a similar reliability threshold next and whether policy or private capital is what pushes it over.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:45.600
The chemist in me wants the next addition measured in gigawatts that can actually shift, not another pretty percentage.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:57.460
I've got panels on the house now so I feel that evening peak question on the bill. Still one country, one year open storage and transmission variables. I would not bet the network on this being the turning point yet.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:05.260
Agreed. We watch the next threshold together. Meanwhile, a completely different kind of recovery posted a number you can count on two hands.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:06.320
The ducks.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:16.700
The ducks, a critically endangered species in a monitored UK wetland just confirmed breeding success. BBC says multiple fledglings made it to independence.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:23.420
How many made it? Multiple fledglings to independence. First time in years.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:35.960
First recruitment count that site has recorded in recent monitoring cycles. That's genuinely wild. Local conservation teams and volunteer wardens held the habitat and ran the predator controls that made it possible.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:51.100
No new gadget. Just people showing up. That's the mechanism and it is almost insultingly simple. Habitat plus predator management. The limiting factor is not a lab result. It is how many sites can get that same consistent attention.

00:03:51.500 --> 00:04:03.900
Here's how this fails in the field. If the predator control depends on volunteers who cannot be at every comparable wetland, you have a single crew problem. I want the exact methods and I want the backup if that crew does not show.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:26.040
You just assigned the ducks a missed approach procedure. Somebody has to. Fair and one successful season does not prove population level recovery. Habitat pressure can erase this. What it does prove is that targeted local protection can produce a visible result inside a single breeding cycle. For a regular person, that's the scale that is actually reachable.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:38.080
The open question is whether those methods scale to more sites without blowing current budgets. If the answer is volunteers forever, I get nervous. If the answer is a repeatable habitat recipe, I get interested.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:52.200
Same pattern, bigger river, the National Fish and Wildlife Foundation, or NFWF, just pointed 1.2 million dollars at restoration along the Pecos River, watershed health and native species recovery.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:02.360
How much? 1.2 million. That's it. For a whole corridor, landowners, state agencies, conservation groups stack together.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:22.780
Huh, that's not a fortune. That's a leverage bet on partners who already live on the river. Water quality, riparian vegetation, conditions for fish and wildlife to rebound, work over the coming seasons. The announcement itself is not an outcome. Success depends on execution and whether the follow-up money actually arrives.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:38.320
So we do not get to celebrate a rebound we have not measured. What I like is the design. Modest per project sums pulling a whole corridor instead of isolated patches. That's how you stop the failure mode where you restore one pretty bend and the next mile stays broken.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:50.120
The open question is how fast restored reaches turn into actual population increases and whether downstream water users see any co-benefit. Watch the counts, not the press release.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:59.880
Same discipline as the ducks count the fledglings then talk. Speaking of showing up when the count is still 0, time to think positive. Who's in the chair?

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:16.420
Angela Duckworth, she describes grit as sustained passion and perseverance toward long-term goals, even when progress feels invisible. Not a mood, a stance. The Australian build, the wetland, the Pecos money, none of those winds arrived in a week.

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:23.920
Does that survive a bad day, a cancelled project, a cockpit at 2 in the morning when nothing is glamorous and you still have a checklist?

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:37.460
That's the test. Choosing to keep showing up on the days when nothing dramatic happens is how you become the person who finishes what matters. The dollars on the Pecos only matter if the partners keep walking back to the river year after year.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.000
So the mental rep today is tiny on purpose.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:50.000
Name the single smallest next action on a project you care about. Completed before you check anything else, that one completion makes the next step feel less optional.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:07:00.200
And if someone is genuinely struggling that rep is not a substitute for real help, that's a professional's job, not a podcast. The rest of us can still do the small thing.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:13.140
If you like the reliability over years angle, Planet Terri and Daily today walks through a review of 51 longevity studies and which biomarkers actually shift. Same patience, different system.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.940
Okay, that brings us to the lever.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:27.520
One regular errand you usually drive. Open a mapping or transit app today, find the public transit or active travel version of that same trip and actually test it this week.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:34.020
First concrete step, pick the errand and write down the scheduled departure time so this is not a vibe.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:47.580
Wait, say the carbon number again. Roughly two to four kilograms of carbon dioxide or CO2 saved per round trip in most mid-sized cities. The cost to you is the time to learn the route.

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.600
Two to four kilograms. That's a small bag of flour you are not thrown into the sky.

00:07:52.600 --> 00:08:04.120
And if even five percent of listeners in car dependent areas tried this once a month, the briefings honest range is thousands of tons a year, depending hard on how good the local transit actually is.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:14.620
I think the second order effect is the real prize. One successful test makes the next errand easier to shift. Trial becomes habit. That's why I'd spend the hour.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:19.340
I'm going to push you. Is this really the highest leverage hour a listener has this week?

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:26.460
In a lot of cities, the bus is late, the bike lane vanishes and you have just donated an afternoon to a science project.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:30.080
Uncertainty about schedules and safety is not imaginary.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:39.060
Steel man accepted. The fix in the numbers is specific. Run the test in daylight on a familiar route first. Then adjust.

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:44.760
The range assumes average urban trip lengths and occupancy, not a fantasy metro.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.400
And check the return trip timing before you commit. Otherwise the experiment creates a new failure mode.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:56.820
Misconnections, a stranded evening, and you never try again. There's your missed approach procedure again.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:59.300
I walked into that.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:08.240
I'll concede this much, writing down the departure time turns a vague good intention into a dispatch. That's the part that actually survives contact with the Tuesday.

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:15.600
And I'll concede the return trip point in your words. If you don't plan the way back, you haven't run a test. You've run a trap.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.840
Still worth the hour if the first trip is chosen so it can succeed.

